From dd71703bd319cd874a60db1b70a70d755d29956e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:35:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Add a microblaze-qemu baseboard

MicroBlaze has no board here, so GCC gets compile-only testing on it.
The nearest thing is baseboards/qemu.exp, which names microblaze-* and
microblazeel-* for QEMU user mode, and whose qemu_load calls "array
size" on local_exec's list return, so it reports pass unconditionally.

This adds a board for QEMU *system* emulation, which needs only a CPU
and a UART rather than the Linux syscall ABI.  It is the usual *-sim
shape, with libgloss_include_flags, newlib_include_flags,
libgloss_link_flags, newlib_link_flags and ldscript "", because libgloss
and newlib already supply the crt0, the linker script, the C library and
the syscalls.  setup_sim is not called: MicroBlaze does have a gdb
simulator, but it implements 107 instructions and cannot cover hard
float, mulh*, pcmp* or lwx/swx, and lwx/swx are emitted at the default
-mcpu, so system emulation is what covers the ISA.

Three things differ from a simulator board.

QEMU cannot return a guest exit status, and libgloss's _exit is "bri 0",
so something has to stop it once the test has reported.  The board sets
needs_status_wrapper and takes the status from testglue.c's "*** EXIT
code N" line as arm-sim.exp does, and contrib/microblaze-qemu-run
watches for that line and then kills QEMU.  The board expects it on PATH
under that name, or named by $SIM as mmixware-sim.exp allows.
sim_time_limit is derived from MB_TIMEOUT so that the outer wait cannot
end up shorter than the wrapper's own.

GCC's MicroBlaze LIB_SPEC links "-lgloss -lxil -lc -lm".  libgloss and
newlib supply three of those.  The fourth is the board's own library,
which nothing upstream builds, because in Xilinx's world it comes from
the tools' board support package, and libgloss's write() and read() are
loops over an outbyte() and inbyte() that are declared and never
defined. microblaze-qemu-libxil.c sits beside testglue.c and supplies
them for the UART; MB_LIBXIL points at the directory holding the
libxil.a built from it.

libgloss's xilinx.ld defaults to text at 0x50, a 1 KiB stack and no
heap, which puts everything inside the 128 KiB of BRAM that
petalogix-s3adsp1800 maps at 0 and leaves malloc failing.  All three are
DEFINED()-guarded, so the board moves text to the RAM at 0x90000000 and
gives the stack and heap 4 MB each.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
